        "note_tweet": {
          "entities": {},
          "text": "In addition, I would like to raise three more concerns that I care deeply about:\n\n7. Unpredictable content distribution and broken creator incentives\nThe recommendation system has become highly unpredictable. Many high-quality, genuinely created posts receive almost zero exposure and are not even seen by real users. Meanwhile, bot-driven engagement distorts the signal, causing the algorithm to misjudge content as “uninteresting.” This makes content creation extremely frustrating and discouraging for serious creators. Over time, this will inevitably damage the platform’s content ecosystem.\n\n8. Arbitrary platform policies and lack of transparency\nCompared to platforms like YouTube, X’s policies feel highly inconsistent and arbitrary. Account suspensions often come without clear explanations or actionable guidance for appeal or correction. Policy directions also appear unstable and difficult to anticipate—sometimes seemingly driven by individual product decisions rather than a consistent framework. For example, the previous attempt at language-based content segregation created confusion and uncertainty among users. A global platform should provide clarity, stability, and predictability.\n\n9. Lack of product focus and poor user support infrastructure\nThere are persistent product issues that remain unresolved for months, such as lag and instability in the article editor, with no visible feedback channel or response from the product team. Meanwhile, features like xChat appear to duplicate existing DM functionality without clear value, while introducing unnecessary delays.\n\nAt the same time, the customer support system is nearly non-functional. It is extremely difficult to reach official support, and most issues—such as wrongful bans—must go through inefficient ticket systems that often receive no response.\n\nWhat is more concerning is the lack of accountability. When questioned, Nikita has stated that the algorithm is not his responsibility. However, as a product manager, he is responsible for the overall product experience. Deflecting responsibility only further erodes user trust."
